WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:08.040
In retrospect, some would say, October 23rd,
1985 was the day the Chinese Communist Party

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.480
died.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.900
By the mid-80s, virtually every inch of the
country had been transformed by well over

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.340
a decade of radical economic reform.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:24.770
But after that day in October, when Deng Xiaoping
explicitly described state policy, as quote,

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:30.080
“Let[ting] some people get rich first”,
the proverbial cat was undeniably and seemingly

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.640
irreversibly out of the bag.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.251
It didn’t matter what ‘-ism’ defined
its economy, or, in the words of Deng, what

00:00:38.251 --> 00:00:45.059
color the cat was, only that it was effective
at catching mice, for ideology had no place

00:00:45.059 --> 00:00:47.780
in this new China.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.989
Class struggle and collectivization had already
given way to new inequalities.

00:00:51.989 --> 00:00:59.399
But with Deng’s unequivocal proclamations,
gone too was even the façade.

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:02.620
There was nothing especially subtle about
it.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.900
Any American visitor to China in the roughly
thirty years after 1980 — myself included

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.640
— inevitably returns with the same surprised
observation: “China looks more Capitalist

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:18.310
than America”, (and that’s saying something!).

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.080
Most iconically, there are the McDonald’s.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.950
So, so many McDonald’s.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.330
And you can’t miss the KFCs.

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:30.770
Twice as many, in fact, as in America.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:34.050
Street vendors everywhere trying to make a
buck.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:38.610
Shopping malls, Apple Stores, and designer
handbags.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:43.800
Chinese consumerism is globally unmatched
in its ostentatiousness.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.960
But the culture of Capitalism has seeped much
deeper than mere brand names.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.580
The American obsession with buying houses
launched the greatest financial crisis of

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:59.470
the 21st century… yet is thoroughly put
to shame by the Chinese, who ‘collect’

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:04.610
third and even fourth homes — all empty,
bought before completion, and likely to never

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:10.539
be lived in by anyone — like the rest of
the world collects stamps.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:13.819
Then there are the class distinctions.

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:16.310
Americans are no strangers to economic inequality.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:22.930
But, at least in theory, no law stops you
from packing up and moving to Beverly Hills.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:28.480
In China, your place of birth quite literally
determines your place in the world.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.170
Household registration — or hukou — means
you can move from middle-of-nowhere Gansu

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:39.110
province to cosmopolitan Shanghai — but
its public schools, hospitals, and jobs will

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:42.870
forever be legally off-limits.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:49.910
Place of birth matters everywhere, but only
in China do authorities not even pretend otherwise.

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:54.540
And if all that doesn’t convince you “Socialism
with Chinese characteristics” is but a euphemism

00:02:54.540 --> 00:03:00.349
for “state Capitalism” there’s plenty
more evidence to choose from…

00:03:00.349 --> 00:03:05.230
Starting in 1980, international corporations
could buy advertising space in the Communist

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:13.090
Party’s official magazine — albeit with
one exception: Coca-Cola was explicitly banned.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:19.580
In the two years between 1986 and 88, student
support for “Communism” fell from 38%

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.680
to just 6.1.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.290
And bear in mind, these are officially-sanctioned
surveys!

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:29.900
Deng Xiaoping even warned that although China
should “maintain vigilance” against the

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:36.480
“Right”, it should be “primarily”
wary of the “Left”.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.680
Since the death of Mao, the Communist Party
has maintained only the thinnest veneer of

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:46.180
tradition in the interest of continuity — acting
out the motions of increasingly-vague 5-year

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.290
plans and increasingly-predictable Politburo
procedure.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:55.260
But while the rest of the world has largely
played along with this political theater,

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:02.280
we’ve all long since realized: underneath
this nearly transparent Maoist packaging is

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:08.690
pure, raw, Big Mac-loving, unadulterated Capitalism
in all but name.

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:12.120
Then, Xi Jinping happened.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:17.600
Today, after a decade of this new era, you
wouldn’t just have to be an optimist to

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:23.590
not see how things have changed, you’d truly
have to be living under a rock.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:29.220
And to notice the similarities between Mao
and Xi is to be confronted with an uncomfortable

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:37.940
question: Did this Maoist revival emerge spontaneously,
implausibly manufactured by one man alone?

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:43.130
Or, did even the most clear-eyed China-watchers
fundamentally misunderstand the past five

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.150
decades?

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:49.110
One way or another, a reappraisal is overdue.

