00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:26.370
On the 9th of September 1976, China experienced
the most traumatic and destabilizing thing

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:32.870
that can happen to a cult of personality — the
death of that personality.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:38.139
With the sole exception, perhaps, of North
Korea’s Kim Il-Sung, no country has ever

00:05:38.139 --> 00:05:42.639
had so many of its political eggs in one basket.

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:44.370
China was the Communist Party.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:48.440
And the Communist Party was Mao.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:54.490
His complete monopoly on power created a volatile
vacuum of power and all but guaranteed a succession

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:58.370
crisis after his fatal heart attack.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:03.750
How you interpret what happened next is where
history diverges.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:08.870
In one version — the one most of us have
come to believe — the death of Mao forced

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:14.150
the Party — indeed, the entire country — to
do some serious soul searching.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:18.729
His absence gave Deng’s generation room
to critically re-examine the horrors of the

00:06:18.729 --> 00:06:22.759
past quarter century and steer the country
in a new direction.

00:06:22.759 --> 00:06:27.920
There’s simply no denying that the scale
and speed of China’s modernization was a

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.480
direct result of these reforms.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:36.860
Had Mao’s 4 confidants not been purged a
mere month after his death, the word “TikTok”

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:40.960
would probably still mean an early-2000s pop
song.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.070
You wouldn’t know what “Shenzhen” is
today.

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:50.110
In a very different version of history, Deng
Xiaoping’s greatest innovation is not liberalizing

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:56.130
the Chinese economy, exactly, but knowing
the secret passwords to join the globalized,

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:58.620
American-led export market.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:02.949
In this world, China’s post-Mao leaders
realize there’s a practical limit to the

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:08.550
amount of starvation the average person is
willing to endure in the name of peasant revolution.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:14.320
They realize, after ‘89, that photographs
of tanks do a lot of damage to the national

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:19.030
brand, and firing squads are bad for business.

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:23.520
At home, China maintains the prominence of
Mao and continues speaking the language of

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.370
Socialism.

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:29.960
But abroad, it emphasizes the “…with Chinese
characteristics” part.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:35.889
It’s a ruse the rest of the world is either
too naive to notice or too blinded by dollar

00:07:35.889 --> 00:07:39.810
signs to act on.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:43.400
The truth, it would seem, is an uneasy mix
of both.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:46.800
Though, probably more of the latter.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.039
On one hand, Mao’s death did allow for the
entry of a new — previously mythical — ‘m’-word

00:07:52.039 --> 00:07:55.710
to the government’s vocabulary — ‘mistake’.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:08:01.020
Accompanying it were ‘democracy’, ‘opposition’,
and even, if you can believe it, ‘human

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:06.520
rights’ — though its precise definition
may have been, shall we say, lost in translation.

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:13.010
Yet, no sooner had Deng uttered these words,
when old red lines were redrawn.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:17.979
In other words, the scope of permissible speech
widened by a remarkable degree in a remarkably

00:08:17.979 --> 00:08:20.600
short period of time.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:26.270
But Deng’s “four cardinal principles”
still left a lot to be desired.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:32.630
In the party’s own words, Mao’s “contributions
to the Chinese revolution far outweigh his

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:33.780
mistakes…”

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:38.510
Another important thing to note — something
we may have dismissed as a mere ‘historical

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:44.750
detail’ for the fifty years between then
and now but today in 2022 is clearly anything

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:49.380
but — is the fact that Mao’s death led
to the very first policy ‘debates’ in

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:51.580
the PRC.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:56.450
For the first time in the country’s history,
you could say things like “Hmm, maybe Mao

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:59.020
was only 70% correct”.

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:01.630
That’s a pretty big deal!

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:04.230
Much bigger than it sounds.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:09.290
That 30% tolerance could be the difference
between, say, China being comfortable importing

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:16.190
foreign-made mRNA vaccines and sealing its
borders shut for years to come.

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:23.640
Anyway, history remembers the victor, and
Deng’s Reformist camp was decisively victorious.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:29.100
But that makes it all too easy to forget that
there were other factions.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:35.350
The more Maoist “Leftist” coalition slowly
faded from the spotlight, but, in hindsight,

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:38.620
never quite disappeared.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:43.300
Leftists couldn’t exactly speak their minds
freely, yet still enjoyed some protection

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:47.380
under the banner of the unassailable “Great
Chairman”.

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:52.450
Censorship is always a cat-and-mouse game
and the more aggressive the cat, the more

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:55.490
clever become the mice.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:59.070
Political groups in China quickly learned
they could get away with just a little bit

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:02.440
more while waving a Little Red Book.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:08.020
Ultimately, Leftists were at the mercy of
the Reformist-led Communist Party, who reluctantly

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:12.250
tolerated their existence, if only as a counterbalance
to the right.

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:17.540
But what Deng couldn’t have predicted was
that several Earth-shattering global crises

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:23.180
many years later would unexpectedly tip the
balance, once again, in the Leftists’ favor

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:29.420
— allowing them to re-emerge from the shadows.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:34.440
However profoundly the fall of the Soviet
Union may have affected the world, it hit

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:38.140
especially close to home in China.

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:43.010
The two countries had long since splintered
ideologically but they still had more in common

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:45.000
than not.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.220
With the Russians out of the picture and no
other large, industrialized role models to

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:55.700
be found, China was effectively left to carry
the mantle of Socialism alone.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:11:01.339
This was uncharted territory and the stakes,
clearly, were existential.

00:11:01.339 --> 00:11:06.820
But what, exactly, was the lesson for Chinese
leaders?

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:12.010
While Deng’s Reformers ‘learned’ that
the Soviets had liberalized too slowly, Leftists

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:17.870
took away the opposite conclusion — that
reform was the problem.

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:24.600
Whatever disagreements lingered, they were
thoroughly put to rest after 2008.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:29.050
Just as the Soviet collapse triggered anxious
introspection and delivered a political win

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:36.610
for the Leftist coalition, so too did the
Global Financial Crisis reverberate in China.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:40.720
While Americans were preoccupied with real
estate and the rest of the world, with its

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:47.500
second, third, and fourth-order effects, China
buckled down for a storm, pumping an unfathomable

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:52.840
amount of credit into its economy in anticipation
of hard times to come.

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:55.120
Shiny airports!

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:56.270
Roads.

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:58.330
Roads to the middle of nowhere.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:00.040
Roads connecting two middle-of-nowheres.

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:07.230
And yes, the colossal High-Speed Rail network
the rest of the world marvels at.

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:11.140
But the looming storm never arrived.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:16.670
Even though many of these roads proved useless
and high-speed trains to the desert sat predictably

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:24.120
empty, when the expected alternative was mass
unemployment, it felt a lot like a victory.

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:28.790
Not only did China emerge from the global
financial crisis unscathed, but it did so

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:32.810
as the only major economy to actually grow.

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:36.120
And by double digits, no less.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:41.440
In just a few short months, Chinese leaders
both lost their reverence for U.S. economic

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:48.200
policy — arguably, U.S. policy period — and
gained immense self-confidence in their own

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:49.400
system.

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:52.990
…Or rather, re-gained.

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:56.800
The last time China stood this tall was under
Mao.

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:01.550
The 2008 Beijing Olympics didn’t hurt, either.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:07.490
Meanwhile, around that same time, the son
of a powerful revolutionary named Bo Xilai

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:13.550
rises to national fame precisely by tapping
into this Maoist nostalgia.

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:17.220
Bo was not a normal Chinese bureaucrat.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:18.220
He was charismatic.

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:20.300
He liked the spotlight.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:25.120
And, most importantly, he was not boring.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:31.180
This highly unusual personal approach to politics
both made him extremely well-liked and probably

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:34.320
led to his ultimate demise.

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:40.860
In short, Bo Xilai was described as a “mini-Mao”,
and his policies as ushering in “Chinese

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:43.089
Socialism 3.0”.

00:13:43.089 --> 00:13:48.360
He built affordable housing, saved struggling
State Enterprises, and helped level the playing

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:51.430
field for poor migrants.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:57.770
Above all, he proved that Socialism, at least
one particular flavor, still had cachet in

00:13:57.770 --> 00:13:59.959
China.

00:13:59.959 --> 00:14:05.779
Not unlike Bernie Sanders or Donald Trump,
his popularity exposed a long-dormant political

00:14:05.779 --> 00:14:07.250
segment.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:11.399
Now, nothing changed overnight.

00:14:11.399 --> 00:14:14.279
New McDonald’s were still being built.

00:14:14.279 --> 00:14:20.389
The iPhone was only beginning to achieve velocity
— largely built with Chinese manufacturing

00:14:20.389 --> 00:14:22.350
and bought by the Chinese middle-class.

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:27.839
But, if you were looking closely, the stage
had been set.

00:14:27.839 --> 00:14:34.420
The Chinese Leftist movement was making a
comeback.

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:40.570
When Xi Jinping inherited power in 2012, he
was reportedly shocked to discover CIA spies

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:43.580
working at the highest levels of the Party.

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:47.350
That, of course, was concerning on its own.

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:51.480
But even more concerning was what that meant.

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:57.260
After three decades of market reform, China
had become near-fatally Capitalist.

00:14:57.260 --> 00:15:02.649
We’re not talking about D.C. lobbyist Crony
Capitalism here.

00:15:02.649 --> 00:15:07.030
There was a second-hand market for Cadre positions.

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:14.639
Let that sink in: you could buy your way into
the Communist party, albeit under the table.

00:15:14.639 --> 00:15:18.399
That was how the CIA allegedly infiltrated
the party.

00:15:18.399 --> 00:15:21.040
They simply paid.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:26.660
And if cadres were willing to sell out their
country that easily, it goes without saying:

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:30.910
the only chairman these corrupt officials
were loyal to was the Mao staring back at

00:15:30.910 --> 00:15:33.350
them on banknotes.

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:38.790
Xi was horrified to find China occupied by
Capitalists.

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:44.740
As we saw in this earlier video, public-private
collusion enabled breakneck economic growth.

00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:49.850
It turns out, the right kind of corruption
can actually speed up development.

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:55.230
But we see now that it also threatened such
necessities to the survival of the Communist

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:58.550
Party as its chain of command.

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:03.959
Xi realized this and launched a multi-year
anti-corruption campaign that took down not

00:16:03.959 --> 00:16:09.730
hundreds or thousands but millions of bureaucrats
across China.

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:13.500
In fact, it never really ended.

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:18.350
Many on the outside interpreted this campaign
through the lens of a dictator accumulating

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:19.350
power.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:25.240
Corruption, after all, is a common pretext
for eliminating one’s rivals.

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:28.990
But this misses the forest for the trees.

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:33.810
Of course Xi Jinping took out some competitors
along the way.

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:38.840
But it’s doubtful he identified a million
and a half competitors.

00:16:38.840 --> 00:16:42.480
And of course he’s trying to consolidate
power.

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:45.019
But to what end?

00:16:45.019 --> 00:16:49.529
A closer look at history reveals that many
of Xi’s signature campaigns began under

00:16:49.529 --> 00:16:53.020
his predecessor, Hu Jintao.

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:58.070
Although the ship was turning too slowly for
many to notice, the new course had long been

00:16:58.070 --> 00:16:59.640
set.

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:03.610
As any Socialist will tell you, Capitalism
becomes entrenched.

00:17:03.610 --> 00:17:09.360
The ‘vanguard’ of the proletariat — cadres
— tend to… lose sight of their comrades

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:12.350
once they own a BMW.

00:17:12.350 --> 00:17:17.649
The difference between Hu and Xi is not so
much ideology but that only the latter has

00:17:17.649 --> 00:17:22.159
the political power to overcome this obstacle.

00:17:22.159 --> 00:17:27.819
Xi Jinping didn’t instigate the Socialist
revival, but he was the first to make it practically

00:17:27.819 --> 00:17:28.819
possible.

00:17:28.819 --> 00:17:34.860
And he doesn’t have much time.

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:41.059
The usual explanation for the Communist Party’s
continued existence amidst a sea of Capitalism

00:17:41.059 --> 00:17:45.539
is “performance legitimacy”, or the implicit
agreement.

00:17:45.539 --> 00:17:51.640
“We’ll make you rich”, it goes, “and
you won’t challenge our authority”.

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:57.030
But perhaps this theory says more about us
than it does about China.

00:17:57.030 --> 00:18:01.690
If you’re really looking, there’s a mountain
of evidence suggesting sincere ideology was

00:18:01.690 --> 00:18:03.230
there all along.

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:09.399
It’s obvious, in retrospect, that reform
had a lifespan.

00:18:09.399 --> 00:18:12.870
Economic growth can’t go on indefinitely.

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:19.830
Even in a country of 1.4 billion, you can
only build so many high-speed trains.

00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:25.450
This places the Party on tenuous ground because
its legitimacy would be at the whim of volatile

00:18:25.450 --> 00:18:29.249
stock markets and real estate bubbles.

00:18:29.249 --> 00:18:34.619
But even if growth did continue, the Communist
Party’s unique claim to power would weaken

00:18:34.619 --> 00:18:37.429
with each passing year.

00:18:37.429 --> 00:18:42.399
While China was “getting rich”, lots of
other countries were getting rich too.

00:18:42.399 --> 00:18:48.070
Reform-era China didn’t have a compelling
source of national pride.

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:52.909
Listen closely and China’s own leaders have
made clear that “making people rich” was

00:18:52.909 --> 00:18:56.149
never its complete strategy.

00:18:56.149 --> 00:19:02.510
This emphasis on economic growth was always
a temporary and partial measure.

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:05.749
Even Reformers believed in some version of
Socialism.

00:19:05.749 --> 00:19:10.519
They just disagreed on how best to get there.

00:19:10.519 --> 00:19:16.330
Although it’s ruled by a Communist Party,
it does not claim to be a Communist State.

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:22.389
Communism, after all, is a Utopian destination
that even its most ardent supporters don’t

00:19:22.389 --> 00:19:26.429
believe is on the horizon anytime soon.

00:19:26.429 --> 00:19:29.350
This is the fundamental source of confusion.

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:36.389
Outsiders, even, or especially, experts not
familiar with Chinese history, see a country

00:19:36.389 --> 00:19:40.519
replete with markets and privately-owned c
ompanies.

00:19:40.519 --> 00:19:46.639
They see, as we did, that even Communist Party
ranks can be bought for cash and conclude

00:19:46.639 --> 00:19:52.580
that “Socialism” is obviously a façade
— a misunderstanding which, ironically,

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:55.330
has suited China quite well.

00:19:55.330 --> 00:20:01.700
But look even closer and it’s clear that
this is itself a façade.

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:06.929
Deng Xiaoping said this explicitly — the
state could never truly distance itself from

00:20:06.929 --> 00:20:10.980
Mao because he was inseparable from the Party.

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:17.029
The writing has been on the wall, if only
we were looking, since 1980 — just 3 years

00:20:17.029 --> 00:20:19.489
after Mao’s death.

00:20:19.489 --> 00:20:24.169
The party was only ever going to deviate from
its origins so much.

00:20:24.169 --> 00:20:30.690
And remember, that was the best case, coming
from Mr. Reform and Opening himself.

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:35.840
Now, there’s some truth to the “implicit
agreement”.

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:41.570
You can easily see how the proletariat would
be more or less patient on the path to Utopia

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:45.710
depending on how many Big Macs they can afford.

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:50.779
But while reform could have been much longer,
shorter, stricter, or looser, depending on

00:20:50.779 --> 00:20:56.950
which coalition won, the existence of the
Communist Party requires that it eventually

00:20:56.950 --> 00:21:00.229
justify its monopoly on power.

00:21:00.229 --> 00:21:04.409
Otherwise, anyone could rule China.

00:21:04.409 --> 00:21:09.510
The other common justification for the party
is the argument that, despite its “flaws”,

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:13.099
it did “lift” 800 million people out of
poverty.

00:21:13.099 --> 00:21:17.220
It’s the retort that any critique of China
is met with.

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:22.009
But there’s just one problem: it’s not
really true.

00:21:22.009 --> 00:21:27.409
I’ve wanted to debunk this myth for years
but the only way to do so honestly is with

00:21:27.409 --> 00:21:33.860
